  "summary": "Four studies directly apply graph neural network-style methods (graph neural networks or learned node embeddings with neighbourhood aggregation) to sepsis early warning or early detection of septic deterioration in ICU settings. Silva et al. (2021) benchmark graph neural networks on the MIMIC-IV dataset for predicting sepsis onset, reporting an AUROC of 0.78 [D0121]. Iyer et al. (2022) use learned node embeddings with neighbourhood aggregation for early detection of septic deterioration, evaluated on the GEFCom dataset, achieving an AUROC of 0.913 [D0122]. Iyer et al. (2021) apply the same neighbourhood aggregation approach specifically to sepsis early warning on the GEFCom dataset, reporting a MAPE of 0.938 [D0123]. Zhou et al. (2025) also employ learned node embeddings with neighbourhood aggregation for early detection of septic deterioration, using the ChurnBench dataset and reporting an AUROC of 0.734 [D0124]. A fifth relevant study, Haddad et al. (2023), addresses early detection of septic deterioration using privacy-preserving federated training on ICU vital signs, reporting accuracy of 0.947 on the ChurnBench dataset [D0127], though it does not explicitly use GNNs.",
